course: dictionary
lesson: 3442
title: Fables and their morals
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A fable is a short story, often with animals that talk, that teaches a lesson. That lesson is called the moral. «In Aesop's fable a shepherd boy cried "Wolf!" for fun so many times that, when a real wolf came, nobody believed him.» The moral is that trust is built on honesty, and once it is broken it is hard to rebuild.
explain: Fables are very old. «In Aesop's fable from ancient Greece, about 2500 years ago, a little mouse saves a lion by gnawing through a net.» Fables are short, so the moral is often easy to find. Ask yourself: what lesson does the animal's story teach?
explain: Fable has other meanings too. It can mean a story that is not true, like a myth or legend. In everyday talk it can mean a lie or made-up claim, as in «His story about the shark was pure fable.» Context clues tell you which meaning fits: is it a story with a lesson, or a claim that is not true?
fact: A fable is a short story, often with animals that talk, that teaches a moral.
fact: The moral is the lesson the fable teaches, and it is often stated at the end.
fact: «Fable» can also mean a story that is not true, like a myth or legend.
fact: In everyday talk, «fable» can mean a lie or made-up claim.
fact: Aesop and La Fontaine are famous writers of fables.
exercise: In a fable, what is the moral? | *The lesson the story teaches | The name of the author | The place where the story is set | The animal who talks the most
exercise: «In Aesop's fable, a little mouse saves a lion by gnawing through a net.» Which animal is the hero of this fable? | The lion | The shepherd | *The mouse | The net
exercise: Amir says a fable is always a true story about real animals. Is he right? | Yes, fables are always true reports from the zoo | Yes, but only if an adult reads it | No, fables never have animals in them | *No, a fable is a short story that teaches a lesson, and it is often not true
test: In «His story about the shark was pure fable», what does fable mean? | A short story with a moral | A map of the sea | A fast swimmer | *A lie or made-up claim
test: A shepherd boy cried "Wolf!" for fun, and then nobody believed him. What is the moral? | Stories with wolves are always funny | *Trust is built on honesty, and once it is broken it is hard to rebuild | Wolves are dangerous animals | Shepherds should always carry a stick
exam: Which of these is a fable? | A recipe for a chocolate cake | *Aesop's story of the mouse who saves a lion | A list of train times | A newspaper report of today's weather
